A||N||17||Monkeyman||Questions for another hairbrained idea||||1||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||08-18-2000||01:37 PM||||I have another stupid idea that needs some questions answered.<P>1)  Can the aux gauges be removed from the housing without damage?<P>2)  Are the plastic carpet supports necessary?<P>3)  As I was removing the console support, I noticed a thin black box inbetween the drivers side carpet support and the "hump".  What's that for??<P>4)  Any suggestions as to alternate vents??<P>5)  What's the best way to cut and finish Plexiglass?  I'm planning on using some lightly smoked Plexiglass, 1/8" thick.  I'll plan on spray painting the back everywhere except where the gauges need to show through.  Hopefully this will make it black, but still let me be able to see the gauges.<P>If anyone has any suggestions, please let me know.  Most of you know how mechanically challenged I am.  This project looks to be more than I'm up to, but I'm never satisfied with stock.  I have some good ideas.  I just hope that I can transform my ideas into reality without it looking like a 3rd graders work.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||08-18-2000||02:53 PM||||It sounds like a good idea.<P><BR>3 - Car alarm maybe?<P>5 - I usually cut it with a saw. (anything with a fine tooth blade usually works. Saber saw or scroll saw lets you make some curves) Then use sand paper to round and smooth the edges a little so it looks like the window glass. Put tape on the plexiglass before you cut it (if it doesn't have the protective covering on it already) to keep from scratching it with the saw.<P>Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||FIEROCIOUS||08-18-2000||03:04 PM||||Monkeyman,<P>1) Yes, four outside screws t15 and two screws inside 7mm. un-clip the wires in the back by pushing the tab.<P>2)I took the plastic out it makes the carpet look horrible so I put them back.<P>3)Dono, what your talking about?<P>4)Are you removeing the center vents?<P>5) I would tape the plexi-glass and use a dremel tool. Cut 1/6 larger than needed and go back and smooth. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||08-18-2000||03:07 PM||||3 - I believe there is a Cruise Control module mounted in that general area.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Taijiguy||08-18-2000||04:55 PM||||Plexi is a "scribe and break" material. They make a special tool, that has what come down to basically being a "barb" on the end. You scribe the plexi with the tool, and snap it off at the scribe.<BR>When scribing the plexiglas, you want to go over it severla times to make sure you get a nice even and fairly deep scratch. Also suppor the "save" piece really well, and SNAP it with confidence, if you try to baby it, it'll bust all to pieces.<BR>I would scribe it in stages, starting with a roughly squared off outline of the shape, and then taking off small pieces until I had the general shape, and then sand or shape to finish. The biggest problem with sawing is that the plexi produces alot of heat, and melts. A belt sander is the best method of rough sanding, since there's enoug surface area on the belt to dissipate the heat.<P>Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000005||hugh||08-18-2000||08:54 PM||||When I built my Chevelle for show,I used Lexan with Marguard.It's more expensive but it does'nt scratch as easy as plexiglas.You should be able to get it at almost any glass place.I used it for a removable rear window so I would have access to the rear engine.I didn't fasten it good enough after one show and it blew out on rt.72 in Long Beach Island,NJ.It was run over by about 20 cars.It got too scratched to use any more,but it didn't break. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hugh_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||HYPTNOTiSE||08-18-2000||09:38 PM||||Number 3 sounds like the amp for the premium sound system to me...||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||08-19-2000||01:46 AM||||Not the amp.  I'm pretty sure that's metal.  This is plastic.  I'll take a pic of it tomorrow if I get a chance.  Probably CC like DRH said.<P>FIEROCIOUS--I can get the harness loose and get the face plate off as well as the metal mounting bracket.  After that, all I see is 2 phillips screws holding the plastic face on.  Is there something under that??  And, yes.  I plan on getting rid of the stock vents.  I was thinking about some of those round ones.  They swivel.  I can't describe them, but I'll look for a pic.<P>Taijiguy--I just found a source for the Plexiglass today and that's what they recommended, too.  Thanx.<P>hugh--I thought about Lexan, but around here it seems to be about 2 1/2 times more expensive.  Since I'm not planning on using it for anything but looks (it'll be completely supported by wood) I figured I'd save a couple of bucks and go with the Plexiglass.<P>Thanx, guys.  I just re read my post and realized that I didn't mention what I was planning.  I'm planning a custom center console.  I'll make a basic shape out of wood, cover the sides with nice vinyl, and put some Plexiglass right down the middle (up the front and back, too).  Nothing terribly exotic or fancy, but something to give the Grape Ape that added touch.  Besides, I never did like the placement of the stereo.  Now I can swap the HVAC and stereo around.  I'll keep everyone informed if I ever get this done.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Taijiguy||08-19-2000||09:34 AM||||That little box is the control module for the cruise, btw. But somebody already said that. I found it in mine too when I was doing some interior work.<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||08-19-2000||11:07 AM||||Thx.  Here's another question.<P>I'm trying to figure out what kind of material to use.  I need some wood for the sides (2 piece from the top of where the aux gauges are to the top of where the ECM cover used to be).  I plan on using layers of the wood for a base, cutting them out to fit the shifter, HVAC, stereo, switches, etc.  I'll cover the wood in the center with smoked Plexiglass or Lexan (maybe I'll spend the extra $$ for Lexan??).  I hear alot about MDF, but none of the lumber yards in town have it.  My thought was to use some pine boards, planed down to the correct thickness.  I thought that might be stronger than plywood.  I need something that is easy to work with as I'm about as mechanically (covers this, too) challenged as they come.  I don't want to do anything with fiberglass.  I've never worked with it and don't want to learn a new trade.  I figure I have about 2 months to get this done.  After that, it will probably be too cold to do much work.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Taijiguy||08-20-2000||09:59 AM||||I'm kinda confused about what you'll be using the wood for. If I understood more clearly what you were trying to accomplish, I could probably help you out there some, since I was a trim carpenter once upon a time. If you are trying to make an image of the sides of the console, running from the top of the gauge pod to the ecm housing, I would recoomend 3/8" plywood. Comparing thickness for thickness, no wood product is as strong as plywood, since it's manufactured with grains that run in different directions. Pine boards would be very subject to corkscrewing and warping from varying heat and humidity levels. Plywood is also subject to that, but it is more pliable, so will conform to a frame better than pine, which would tend to try to follow its preferred shape, distorting anything less than a substantial frame. MDF, or particle board is more stable than plywood or pine boards, and of the three is cheaper. (pine boards would be outrageous if you're doing what I think you are) Particle board can be bought in thicknesses to 3/8" and it's pretty easy to work with.<BR>The one thing to keep in mind, if you are in fact making the console out of wood, is you have to make a hell of a strong frame, that's going to be the primary factor in whether this project works or not. If the frame is good, the rest will fall in place. And if you AREN'T doing what I've been talking about, then I just wasted a whole buttload of bandwidth on an irrelivent explanation!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>-Taiji<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||08-20-2000||11:00 AM||||Taijiguy--I was planning a custom console from dash to ECM.  Now that I've given it some thought, I think I'll actually do an entire dash.  The lumber yard recommended 1/2" AC fir plywood for strength and finish.  I made the mistake of reading an old issue of AS&S (Auto Sound & Security) and now I'm caught up in the smoked Plexi/Lexan and stealth stuff.  I spent a couple of hours last night thinking about this and came up with a very crude drawing of what I want.  Basically, I'd like to make the front of the dash (the side facing the driver/passenger) flat, but even on both sides.  I'll cover the faces with the Plexi/Lexan.  The center console will be slightly higher than the dash, then pretty much one piece all the way back to the ECM.  (I'll have to make it more than one piece somewhere probably for removal.)  I'd like to hide a Dakota Digital (are there other companies that make the same?) speedo/tach behind the top portion of the center console (heck, you can't see them in their original position anyways) and the rest of the gauges (same source) behind the drivers side panel, micro switches for the trunk release, headlights, etc.  One piece at a slight angle from the top front of the center console to the front of the gear shifter.  I can't seem to get my scanner working this am, so I can't post a scan of my "drawing".  If you really are interested in giving me some hints, feel free to email me.  I know what shape I want, it's just a matter of working out some details and finding out if certain parts will work.  For example, can I really use micro switches (small, rocker type, less than 1" tall) for everything?  Is there something I could use that would be more stealthy?  BTW, I plan on covering the top, bottom and sides with a good grade of vinyl.  <P>This is getting too long.  Email me and I'll try to scan a drawing.  Then you can advise me.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Taijiguy||08-21-2000||09:21 AM||||I'll email you later when I get a few minutes. I will say that for the switches, I would go with a flat panel of switches. Like something salvaged from an old microwave oven! If you can find one old enough, they had a very smooth control panel, that was touch sensitive. You could trace out the wires, and use either solid state (transistor) switches, or realys, or a combination of both. You could paint over the face, and have some (I forget who from here does that kind of thing)  make up some graphics you could install over the switch positions. I did that on a Kelmark I owned and have thought about doing it with my Fiero. It is COOL!<BR>I'll email in a few...<P>-Taiji<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 08-21-2000).]||||reg||1
